Standardized UDTs and AOIs are the foundation of the WHK unified control philosophy, ensuring every piece of equipment and process follows identical patterns across Grain Loading, Milling, Mashing, Fermentation, Distillation, Distillate Processing, and Warehousing.

**CORE UDT ARCHITECTURE**:

**1. BASE EQUIPMENT UDT - Motor Control**:

```
// UDT_MOTOR - Universal motor control structure
TYPE UDT_Motor
STRUCT
    // === COMMANDS ===
    Command : STRUCT
        Start : BOOL;
        Stop : BOOL;
        Reset : BOOL;
    END_STRUCT;

    // === FEEDBACK ===
    Feedback : STRUCT
        Running : BOOL;
        Stopped : BOOL;
        Fault : BOOL;
        Overload : BOOL;
    END_STRUCT;

    // === MODES (Standard across ALL equipment) ===
    Mode : STRUCT
        Off : BOOL;
        Manual : BOOL;
        Auto : BOOL;
        Hold : BOOL;
        CIP : BOOL;          // Clean-in-place
        Current : DINT;       // 0=Off, 1=Manual, 2=Auto, 3=Hold, 4=CIP
    END_STRUCT;

    // === MODE CONFIGURATION (Enable/Disable per instance) ===
    Config : STRUCT
        Allow_Manual : BOOL := TRUE;   // Set FALSE if manual not allowed
        Allow_Auto : BOOL := TRUE;
        Allow_Hold : BOOL := TRUE;
        Allow_CIP : BOOL := FALSE;     // Most motors don't need CIP mode
    END_STRUCT;

    // === PERMISSIVES/INTERLOCKS ===
    Permissives : STRUCT
        All_OK : BOOL;                  // AND of all permissives
        Emergency_Stop_Clear : BOOL;
        Equipment_Ready : BOOL;
        // Add up to 16 permissives
    END_STRUCT;

    Interlocks : STRUCT
        Trip_Active : BOOL;             // OR of all trip conditions
        Overload_Trip : BOOL;
        Low_Flow_Trip : BOOL;
        // Add trip conditions as needed
    END_STRUCT;

    // === ALARMS ===
    Alarms : STRUCT
        Active : BOOL;                  // Any alarm active
        High_Current : BOOL;
        Runtime_Exceeded : BOOL;
    END_STRUCT;

    // === METRICS/TOTALIZATION ===
    Metrics : STRUCT
        Runtime_Hours : REAL;           // Totalizer
        Start_Count : DINT;             // Cycle counter
        Last_Start_Time : DATE_AND_TIME;
        Last_Stop_Time : DATE_AND_TIME;
    END_STRUCT;

    // === METADATA (For AI/ML context) ===
    Metadata : STRUCT
        Equipment_ID : STRING[32];      // e.g., "MTR-ML-001"
        Description : STRING[80];        // e.g., "Grain Mill Motor"
        Location : STRING[40];           // e.g., "Milling Area"
        Process : STRING[20];            // e.g., "Milling"
    END_STRUCT;
END_STRUCT;
END_TYPE

// USAGE ACROSS ALL PROCESSES:

// Milling Process
Mill_Motor_01 : UDT_Motor;
Mill_Motor_01.Metadata.Equipment_ID := "MTR-ML-001";
Mill_Motor_01.Metadata.Process := "Milling";
Mill_Motor_01.Config.Allow_CIP := FALSE;  // No CIP for mill motor

// Mashing Process
Mash_Agitator_01 : UDT_Motor;
Mash_Agitator_01.Metadata.Equipment_ID := "MTR-MSH-001";
Mash_Agitator_01.Metadata.Process := "Mashing";
Mash_Agitator_01.Config.Allow_CIP := TRUE;  // Agitator can run in CIP

// Fermentation Process
Fermenter_Agitator_F01 : UDT_Motor;
Fermenter_Agitator_F01.Metadata.Equipment_ID := "MTR-FERM-F01";
Fermenter_Agitator_F01.Metadata.Process := "Fermentation";

// ALL three motors have IDENTICAL structure!
// Different configurations, but same data schema
```

**2. VALVE CONTROL UDT**:

```
TYPE UDT_Valve
STRUCT
    Command : STRUCT
        Open : BOOL;
        Close : BOOL;
    END_STRUCT;

    Feedback : STRUCT
        Fully_Open : BOOL;
        Fully_Closed : BOOL;
        Position_Percent : REAL;  // For modulating valves
        Traveling : BOOL;
    END_STRUCT;

    Mode : STRUCT  // Same as Motor!
        Off : BOOL;
        Manual : BOOL;
        Auto : BOOL;
        Current : DINT;
    END_STRUCT;

    Config : STRUCT
        Valve_Type : DINT;  // 0=On/Off, 1=Modulating
        Fail_Position : DINT;  // 0=Closed, 1=Open, 2=Last
        Stroke_Time : REAL;  // Seconds to fully stroke
    END_STRUCT;

    // ... Permissives, Alarms, Metrics, Metadata (same pattern)
END_STRUCT;
END_TYPE

// Used in FCV-4032 (Still01 cooling water valve)
FCV_4032 : UDT_Valve;
FCV_4032.Config.Valve_Type := 1;  // Modulating (0-100%)
FCV_4032.Config.Fail_Position := 0;  // Fail Closed (safety)
```

**3. UNIT PROCEDURE UDT** (for Batch Control):

```
TYPE UDT_Unit_Procedure
STRUCT
    // === STATE MACHINE (ISA-88/106 States) ===
    State : STRUCT
        Current : DINT;          // 0=IDLE, 1=STARTING, 2=RUNNING, etc.
        Target : DINT;           // Commanded state
        Previous : DINT;         // For transition tracking
        Names : ARRAY[0..8] OF STRING;  // ["IDLE", "STARTING", ...]
    END_STRUCT;

    // === PHASE/SEQUENCE ===
    Phase : STRUCT
        Active : STRING[40];     // Current phase name
        Step : DINT;             // Current step number
        Step_Name : STRING[40];  // Step description
    END_STRUCT;

    // === BATCH CONTEXT (ISA-95 Integration) ===
    Batch : STRUCT
        ID : STRING[40];         // e.g., "MASH-2025-10-03-001"
        Recipe_ID : STRING[40];  // Recipe being executed
        Product_Code : STRING[20];
        Start_Time : DATE_AND_TIME;
        End_Time : DATE_AND_TIME;
        Duration : REAL;         // Minutes
        Operator_ID : STRING[20];
        Shift : DINT;            // 1=Day, 2=Evening, 3=Night
    END_STRUCT;

    // === SEQUENCE CONTROL ===
    Control : STRUCT
        Hold_Requested : BOOL;
        Abort_Requested : BOOL;
        Resume_Requested : BOOL;
        Skip_Step_Requested : BOOL;
    END_STRUCT;

    // === KPIs (Process-Specific Calculated Results) ===
    KPIs : STRUCT
        Yield_Percent : REAL;
        Quality_Index : REAL;
        Energy_Used : REAL;      // kWh or BTU
        Efficiency : REAL;
        // Extensible - add process-specific KPIs
    END_STRUCT;
END_STRUCT;
END_TYPE

// USAGE:

// Mashing Unit
Mash_Tun_01_Procedure : UDT_Unit_Procedure;
Mash_Tun_01_Procedure.Batch.ID := "MASH-2025-10-03-001";
Mash_Tun_01_Procedure.Phase.Active := "Saccharification";
Mash_Tun_01_Procedure.KPIs.Yield_Percent := 78.5;  // Extract yield

// Fermentation Unit
Fermenter_F01_Procedure : UDT_Unit_Procedure;  // SAME UDT!
Fermenter_F01_Procedure.Batch.ID := "FERM-2025-10-03-001";
Fermenter_F01_Procedure.Phase.Active := "Fermenting";
Fermenter_F01_Procedure.KPIs.Yield_Percent := 92.0;  // Attenuation

// Distillation Unit
Still_01_Procedure : UDT_Unit_Procedure;  // SAME UDT!
Still_01_Procedure.Batch.ID := "DIST-2025-10-03-001";
Still_01_Procedure.Phase.Active := "Hearts Collection";
Still_01_Procedure.KPIs.Yield_Percent := 85.0;  // Proof yield
```

**AOI IMPLEMENTATION**:

**Motor Control AOI**:
```
ADD_ON_INSTRUCTION Motor_Control
VAR_INPUT
    Enable : BOOL;
END_VAR

VAR_IN_OUT
    Motor : UDT_Motor;  // Motor UDT instance
END_VAR

VAR_OUTPUT
    Run_Command : BOOL;  // To motor starter
END_VAR

// LOGIC:
IF Motor.Command.Start AND 
   Motor.Permissives.All_OK AND 
   NOT Motor.Interlocks.Trip_Active AND
   Motor.Config.Allow_Auto THEN

    Motor.Mode.Current := 2;  // Auto mode
    Run_Command := TRUE;

    // Increment metrics
    IF NOT Motor.Feedback.Running THEN  // Transition to running
        Motor.Metrics.Start_Count += 1;
        Motor.Metrics.Last_Start_Time := Current_DateTime();
    END_IF;
END_IF;

// Runtime totalizer
IF Motor.Feedback.Running THEN
    Motor.Metrics.Runtime_Hours += DeltaT / 3600;  // Convert to hours
END_IF;

END_ADD_ON_INSTRUCTION;

// This AOI is used for EVERY motor across ALL seven processes
// Guarantees identical behavior and data collection
```

**BENEFITS OF STANDARDIZATION**:

**For Control Engineers**:
- Write once, use everywhere
- Changes to AOI apply to all instances
- Clear, maintainable code

**For Operators**:
- Same faceplate for all motors/valves/units
- Consistent mode switching across plant
- Reduced training time

**For AI/ML**:
- Identical data structure across all equipment
- No special-case parsing needed
- Cross-process learning possible
- Equipment comparison straightforward

**CONFIGURATION FLAGS** (Not Structural Changes):

**Wrong Approach**:
```
// DON'T create different UDTs for different processes
UDT_Motor_Milling  // Has fields X, Y, Z
UDT_Motor_Fermentation  // Has fields X, Y (missing Z!)
// Now data structures are incompatible!
```

**Correct Approach**:
```
// ONE UDT with configuration flags
UDT_Motor  // Has fields X, Y, Z for ALL instances

// Configure per instance:
Mill_Motor.Config.Allow_CIP := FALSE;  // Z feature disabled
Ferm_Motor.Config.Allow_CIP := TRUE;   // Z feature enabled

// Field Z still EXISTS in both (data structure identical)
// Just configured differently
```

**WHY THIS MATTERS**:
```
// AI can query ALL motors with one schema
SELECT * FROM Motors WHERE Process = 'Milling';
SELECT * FROM Motors WHERE Metrics.Runtime_Hours > 1000;
SELECT AVG(Metrics.Start_Count) FROM Motors GROUP BY Process;

// Works because ALL motors have identical UDT structure
// Regardless of process or configuration
```

**Best Practice**: Design UDTs to be comprehensive (include all possible fields for any use case). Use Config booleans to enable/disable features per instance. Never modify UDT structure for individual processes - preserve schema uniformity.

ISA-88 state-based control provides a universal framework for all distillery processes, creating consistent operation AND structured data for AI/ML. Here's the complete implementation strategy.

**UNIVERSAL STATE MODEL**:

**Standard States for ALL Units** (Mashing, Fermentation, Distillation, etc.):
```
0 = IDLE        // Stopped, ready for next batch
1 = STARTING    // Transition to operation (pre-checks, warm-up)
2 = RUNNING     // Normal production operation
3 = PAUSED/HOLD // Temporarily suspended (can resume)
4 = STOPPING    // Controlled shutdown sequence
5 = COMPLETED   // Batch/operation finished successfully
6 = ABORTED     // Emergency stop or fault condition
7 = CIP         // Clean-in-place sequence
8 = FAULT       // Equipment fault (requires intervention)
```

**IMPLEMENTATION IN PLC**:

```
// Universal Unit Procedure UDT (from Control Philosophy)
TYPE UDT_Unit_State_Machine
STRUCT
    State : STRUCT
        Current : DINT := 0;              // Current state (0=IDLE initially)
        Previous : DINT := 0;             // For transition tracking
        Target : DINT := 0;               // Commanded next state
        Transition_Time : DATE_AND_TIME;  // When last changed
        Duration_Current : REAL;          // Time in current state (min)
    END_STRUCT;

    // State names for HMI display
    State_Names : ARRAY[0..8] OF STRING := 
        ["IDLE", "STARTING", "RUNNING", "HOLD", "STOPPING", 
         "COMPLETED", "ABORTED", "CIP", "FAULT"];

    // Transition commands
    Commands : STRUCT
        Start : BOOL;     // IDLE → STARTING
        Hold : BOOL;      // RUNNING → HOLD
        Resume : BOOL;    // HOLD → RUNNING
        Stop : BOOL;      // Any → STOPPING → IDLE
        Abort : BOOL;     // Any → ABORTED
        Reset : BOOL;     // FAULT/ABORTED → IDLE
        StartCIP : BOOL;  // IDLE → CIP
    END_STRUCT;
END_STRUCT;
END_TYPE
```

**STATE MACHINE LOGIC** (Reusable across ALL processes):

```
// Generic state transition logic (same for all units)
CASE Unit.State.Current OF

0: // IDLE STATE
    IF Unit.Commands.Start AND All_Permissives_OK THEN
        Unit.State.Target := 1;  // → STARTING
        Log_Event("Unit transitioning IDLE → STARTING");
    ELSIF Unit.Commands.StartCIP THEN
        Unit.State.Target := 7;  // → CIP
    END_IF;

1: // STARTING STATE
    // Execute startup sequence
    IF Startup_Complete THEN
        Unit.State.Target := 2;  // → RUNNING
        Log_Event("Unit transitioning STARTING → RUNNING");
    ELSIF Fault_Detected THEN
        Unit.State.Target := 8;  // → FAULT
    END_IF;

2: // RUNNING STATE
    // Normal operation
    IF Unit.Commands.Hold THEN
        Unit.State.Target := 3;  // → HOLD
    ELSIF Unit.Commands.Stop THEN
        Unit.State.Target := 4;  // → STOPPING
    ELSIF Unit.Commands.Abort OR Critical_Interlock THEN
        Unit.State.Target := 6;  // → ABORTED
    ELSIF Fault_Detected THEN
        Unit.State.Target := 8;  // → FAULT
    ELSIF Batch_Complete THEN
        Unit.State.Target := 5;  // → COMPLETED
    END_IF;

3: // HOLD STATE
    IF Unit.Commands.Resume THEN
        Unit.State.Target := 2;  // → RUNNING
    ELSIF Unit.Commands.Stop THEN
        Unit.State.Target := 4;  // → STOPPING
    END_IF;

// ... Similar logic for states 4-8

END_CASE;

// Execute state transition
IF Unit.State.Target <> Unit.State.Current THEN
    Unit.State.Previous := Unit.State.Current;
    Unit.State.Current := Unit.State.Target;
    Unit.State.Transition_Time := Current_DateTime();
    Unit.State.Duration_Current := 0.0;

    // Log transition for AI/ML
    Log_State_Transition({
        Unit_ID: Unit.Metadata.Equipment_ID,
        From_State: State_Names[Previous],
        To_State: State_Names[Current],
        Timestamp: Transition_Time,
        Batch_ID: Unit.Batch.ID,
        Operator: Current_User
    });
END_IF;

// Update duration in current state
Unit.State.Duration_Current += DeltaT / 60;  // Minutes
```

**PROCESS-SPECIFIC APPLICATIONS**:

**Mashing Process**:
```
Mash_Tun_01 : UDT_Unit_State_Machine;

STATE_IDLE:
    // Waiting for next batch

STATE_STARTING:
    Phase := "Fill Water";
    // Execute water fill sequence

STATE_RUNNING:
    // Execute mash schedule phases
    CASE Mash_Phase OF
        1: Phase := "Protein Rest" (122°F);
        2: Phase := "Saccharification" (152°F);
        3: Phase := "Mash Out" (168°F);
    END_CASE;

STATE_COMPLETED:
    // Mash complete, ready for transfer
```

**Fermentation Process**:
```
Fermenter_F01 : UDT_Unit_State_Machine;  // SAME UDT!

STATE_IDLE:
    // Clean, ready for wort

STATE_STARTING:
    Phase := "Filling";
    // Transfer wort from mash tun

STATE_RUNNING:
    Phase := "Fermenting";
    // Maintain 68-72°F (using IMC-tuned TIC-F01)
    // Monitor attenuation

STATE_COMPLETED:
    // Fermentation finished (target FG reached)
```

**Distillation Process (Still01)**:
```
Still_01 : UDT_Unit_State_Machine;  // SAME UDT!

STATE_IDLE:
    // Still cold, ready for charge

STATE_STARTING:
    Phase := "Heat Up";
    // Ramp to operating temperature

STATE_RUNNING:
    // Execute distillation phases
    CASE Distillation_Phase OF
        1: Phase := "Foreshots" (discard);
        2: Phase := "Heads" (methanol rich);
        3: Phase := "Hearts" (ethanol - collect);
        4: Phase := "Tails" (fusel oils);
    END_CASE;
    // Cascade control active (TIC-Vent-4031, TIC-Product-4033)

STATE_COMPLETED:
    // Distillation run complete
```

**AI/ML BENEFITS FROM STANDARDIZED STATES**:

**1. Consistent Event Data**:
```
// ALL units log transitions identically
Query: SELECT * FROM State_Transitions 
       WHERE To_State = 'RUNNING' 
       AND Process IN ('Mashing', 'Fermentation', 'Distillation');

// Returns uniform data:
// - Same schema across all processes
// - Same state codes
// - Same timestamp format
// - Same batch ID linking
```

**2. Cycle Time Analysis**:
```
// Measure time in each state across all batches
SELECT Process, State, AVG(Duration) as Avg_Minutes
FROM State_Duration_Log
GROUP BY Process, State;

// Identify bottlenecks:
// - Which state takes longest?
// - Which process has most variability?
// - Where to optimize?
```

**3. Fault Pattern Recognition**:
```
// ML model learns precursors to ABORTED state
Training_Data := {
    Features: [Temp_Trend, Pressure_Trend, Time_In_Running, etc.],
    Label: "Transitioned_To_Abort" (1 or 0)
};

// Model predicts: "Fermenter F02 likely to abort in next 2 hours"
// Because temperature trend similar to past aborts
```

**4. Cross-Process Learning**:
```
// ML trained on Mashing can transfer to Fermentation
// Because both use:
// - Same state codes
// - Same UDT structure
// - Same event logging
// - Similar process dynamics (both thermal)

// Transfer learning reduces training data needed
```

**5. Production Optimization**:
```
// Analyze which state durations correlate with best yield
Optimal_Saccharification_Time := ML_Model(
    Historical_Data: [
        Time_In_Saccharification_State,
        Temp_Profile,
        Final_Extract_Yield
    ]
);

// Recommend: "Extend saccharification by 10 min for 3% yield increase"
```

